forestation due to oll palm, we analysed recent expansions\nand modelled likely future ones. We assessed sample areas to find where oll palm planta-\ntions have recently replaced forests in 20 countries, using a combination of high-resolution\nimagery from Google Earth and Landsat. We then compared these trends to countrywide\n{ends in FAO data for ol palm planted area. Finally, we assessed which forests have high\nagricultural suitability for future ol palm development, which we refer to as vulnerable for-\n\u2018ests, and identified crtcal areas for biodiversity that ol palm expansion threatens. Our anal-\nysis reveals regional trends in deforestation associated with ol palm agriculture. In\nSoutheast Asia, 45% of sampled oll palm plantations came from areas that were forests in\n1989, For South America, the percentage was 31%. By contrast, in Mesoamerica and\n\u2018Altica, we observed only 2% and 7% of ol palm plantations coming from areas that were\nforestin 1989. The largest areas of vulnerable forest are in Arica and South America. Vul-\nerable forests in all four regions of production contain globally high concentrations of mam-\nmal and bird species at risk of extinction. However, priority areas for biodiversity\n\u2018conservation difer based on taxa and criteria used. Government regulation and voluntary\nmarket interventions can help incentvize the expansion of ol palm plantations in ways that\nprotect biodiversity ch ecosystems.\n\nIntroduction\nAfrican oil palm (Elacis guincensis Jacq.) isa tropical crop grown primarily for the production\n\u2018of palm ol. Itis the world\u2019s highest yielding and least expensive vegetable oi, making tthe pre-\nferred cooking ol for millions of people globally and a source of biodiesel. Palm oil and its\n<erivatives are also common ingredients in many packaged and fast foods, personal care and\n\nPLOS ONE |D01:10.1971/}oumalpone.0158668 July 27,2016 1/19",
